# Practical: Dockerize an Application and Deploy It in a Container

We'll create a simple **web application**, package it into a Docker image, and run it inside a Docker container.

The complete flow is:

```text
Application
    ↓
Dockerfile
    ↓
docker build
    ↓
Docker Image
    ↓
docker run
    ↓
Docker Container
    ↓
Open application in browser
```

We'll use a simple **HTML web application with Nginx**, so you don't need Node.js, Java, or Python.

---

## Step 1 — Make sure Docker is running

Open Docker Desktop and make sure it is running.

Then open Command Prompt/PowerShell and execute:

```bash
docker --version
```

You should see something like:

```text
Docker version 2x.x.x
```

Also test:

```bash
docker run hello-world
```

If you see Docker's welcome message, Docker is working. ✅

---

# Step 2 — Create the application folder

Create a folder:

```bash
mkdir docker-web-app
cd docker-web-app
```

Your folder will initially be:

```text
docker-web-app/
```

---

# Step 3 — Create the web application

Inside `docker-web-app`, create:

```text
index.html
```

Put this code inside:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Docker Web Application</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Docker!</h1>
    <p>This web application is running inside a Docker container.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

Save the file.

Your project is now:

```text
docker-web-app/
└── index.html
```

---

# Step 4 — Create the Dockerfile

In the same folder, create a file named:

```text
Dockerfile
```

**Make sure it is exactly `Dockerfile`, not `Dockerfile.txt`.**

Put this inside:

```dockerfile
FROM nginx:alpine

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

EXPOSE 80
```

Save it.

Now your project is:

```text
docker-web-app/
│
├── Dockerfile
└── index.html
```

---

# Step 5 — Understand the Dockerfile

### `FROM`

```dockerfile
FROM nginx:alpine
```

Uses the lightweight Nginx web server image as the base.

### `COPY`

```dockerfile
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
```

Copies our HTML file into the location where Nginx serves web pages.

### `EXPOSE`

```dockerfile
EXPOSE 80
```

Documents that the application uses port **80** inside the container.

---

# Step 6 — Build the Docker image

Make sure you are inside:

```text
docker-web-app
```

Run:

```bash
docker build -t docker-web-app .
```

The command means:

```text
docker build
      ↓
build an image

-t docker-web-app
      ↓
give the image the name "docker-web-app"

.
      ↓
use the current directory
```

Wait for the build to complete.

You should eventually see something similar to:

```text
Successfully tagged docker-web-app:latest
```

Depending on your Docker version, the output may look slightly different.

---

# Step 7 — Check the Docker image

Run:

```bash
docker images
```

You should see:

```text
REPOSITORY       TAG       IMAGE ID       SIZE
docker-web-app   latest    xxxxxxxx       ...
```

Your application has now been converted into a Docker image. ✅

---

# Step 8 — Create and start the container

Now run:

```bash
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name my-web-app docker-web-app
```

Let's understand it:

```text
-d
```

Runs the container in the background.

```text
-p 8080:80
```

Connects:

```text
Your computer port 8080
          ↓
Container port 80
```

```text
--name my-web-app
```

Gives the container the name `my-web-app`.

```text
docker-web-app
```

Is the image from which the container is created.

---

# Step 9 — Check whether the container is running

Run:

```bash
docker ps
```

You should see something similar to:

```text
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE            PORTS
xxxxxxxx       docker-web-app   0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp
```

This means your container is running. 🎉

---

# Step 10 — Open the application

Open your browser.

Go to:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

You should see:

# Hello from Docker!

and:

> This web application is running inside a Docker container.

🎉 **Your web application is now deployed in a Docker container.**

---

# Step 11 — Check container logs

You can see what Nginx is doing with:

```bash
docker logs my-web-app
```

After visiting the website, you may see HTTP requests in the logs.

---

# Step 12 — Stop the container

When you want to stop the application:

```bash
docker stop my-web-app
```

Check:

```bash
docker ps
```

The container will no longer appear among running containers.

---

# Step 13 — Start it again

You don't need to build the image again.

Simply run:

```bash
docker start my-web-app
```

Then open:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

The application is running again. ✅

---

# Step 14 — Modify the application

Open:

```text
index.html
```

Change:

```html
<h1>Hello from Docker!</h1>
```

to:

```html
<h1>My First Dockerized Application</h1>
```

Save it.

Now **important**: the existing container contains the old version of the application.

So rebuild the image:

```bash
docker build -t docker-web-app .
```

Then remove the old container:

```bash
docker rm -f my-web-app
```

Create a new container:

```bash
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name my-web-app docker-web-app
```

Refresh:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

You should see the updated page.

---

# Step 15 — See all containers

Run:

```bash
docker ps -a
```

This shows both running and stopped containers.

For example:

```text
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE            STATUS
xxxxxxxx       docker-web-app   Up ...
yyyyyyyy       docker-web-app   Exited ...
```

---

# Step 16 — Remove the container

When finished:

```bash
docker stop my-web-app
```

Then:

```bash
docker rm my-web-app
```

---

# Step 17 — Remove the image

If you also want to remove the image:

```bash
docker rmi docker-web-app
```

Check:

```bash
docker images
```

---

# ⭐ Most important commands

For your practical, remember these:

